1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now, the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
In 1997, I worked at the jamboree.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
It was something called a hospitality officer for the Northeast region.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
One o'clock in the afternoon, here comes this little tag, walking up this trail.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
He looked pretty sad.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
And he walked in, looked at my name tag, said,

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Mr. Butler, could you help me?

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
And I said, well, sure, try what's up.

9
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,000
He says, I lost my wallet.

10
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
I said, how much money did you have?

11
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
He said, $150.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
So I said, well, okay, tell me what troop you're in

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,000
and all that business.

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
We'll see what we can do about finding it and getting it back to you.

15
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
And he left and I thought, you know what?

16
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
There's probably very little chance that we're going to find that boy's wallet.

17
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
At three o'clock, here comes another kid into this tent.

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
So my name tag says, Mr. Butler, have gone to wallet.

19
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
And you know what?

20
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
When I tell their store, show them can I do it and remain dry eyed.

21
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Because that boy handed me this black wallet that had $150.

22
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
And, you know, when people wonder why we do this,

23
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
they wonder if it's got any pay off.

24
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,000
They wonder if it works.

25
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
It will work.

26
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Hey, this is podcast number 85.

27
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Hey, this is Clark Green.

28
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Welcome back to the Scalemaster podcast.

29
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Let's take a look at the mailbag here.

30
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
First of all, let me apologize.

31
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
If I haven't gotten back to you very quickly with emails the past week,

32
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
it's been a week.

33
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Do I have to say anymore?

34
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
It's September, things are heating up and programs are running

35
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
and meetings are happening.

36
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Oh my gosh, how many meetings?

37
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Well, so anyways, I did hear from Brit Helms.

38
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Brit is an assistant scout master in troop 108 in Fort Mill, South Carolina.

39
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
And he had this to say, first of all, thanks for your podcast.

40
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,000
I've been listening since about episode four and loving each and every one of them.

41
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
I highly recommend them to the other leaders of my troop.

42
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
I know the insights experience.

43
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
And yes, even the jokes keep me wanting to help my scouts.

44
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,000
I'm glad the jokes are having that effect on somebody.

45
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
That's all I can say.

46
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Keep me wanting to help my scouts achieve more in our troops specifically

47
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
and in scouting in general.

48
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
I'll turn 48 next month.

49
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
And I have been a registered scout or scouter for 40 of those years.

50
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Earlier this year, I had the distinct pleasure of having my own

51
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Eagle Scout Award from 1979 presented to my son during his Eagle Court of Honor.

52
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Well, that's wonderful, Brit.

53
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
That's got to be quite a moment.

54
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
And congratulations, by the way.

55
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
He's the first second generation Eagle in troop 108.

56
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:29,000
That being an Eagle Scout, whose father also received his Eagle in the same troop.

57
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
I couldn't be more proud.

58
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Oh, that's wonderful.

59
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
By day, I'm an IT man.

60
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
And, but after my normal work day when I'm not scouting,

61
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
I also work as a voiceover artist from my home studio.

62
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
I have heard Baden Powell's comments on each of your podcasts.

63
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
And I wondered if I could put some of my voiceover skills to work for you

64
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
as an appreciation for what you've done for me through your podcast.

65
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Well, that's a very kind thing to have done.

66
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
One of the techniques used in voiceover is called voice matching.

67
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:08,000
And it's one of those things I enjoy doing most, but get to do the least.

68
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
I've attached a little thank you that I hope you will see fit to use in your podcast.

69
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And I will play that for you now.

70
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
I will play that for you now.

71
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Here we go.

72
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Ready?

73
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
This is what Brit sent me.

74
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
I highly recommend the Scout Master Podcast.

75
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
I mean, what more could you ask for?

76
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
That's great.

77
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
The recordings of Baden Powell that I play on the podcast that you hear

78
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
are little snippets edited out of some longer recordings that you can find online.

79
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And we hear from Baden Powell in the beginning of the program.

80
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
He's the one who says, and now to use Scout Masters.

81
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
And then we hear from him at the end where he wishes us good luck and good camping.

82
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
So now he's also going to recommend the Scout Master Podcast.

83
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
That is just great.

84
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
He goes on to say, at first I contemplated whether or not to do this

85
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
and some might think it's sacrilege to voice match Baden Powell.

86
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
But I feel in my heart of hearts that if he were here,

87
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
he would indeed highly recommend the Scout Master Podcast.

88
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
I feel that in my heart of hearts too, Brit.

89
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
I'm not too humble to say that.

90
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
But wow, what a very nice email.

91
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
I really appreciate the message.

92
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
I appreciate the gift and everybody gets to hear that now.

93
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
It'll be towards the end of the podcast, you know,

94
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
where we say it's time to hear from our founder.

95
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Yeah, I think I'm bold enough to put that in there.

96
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:46,000
And once again, congratulations on your son having reached Eagle Scout.

97
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
And keep listening.

98
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Thank you for all the kind words.

99
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Well, in this edition of the Scout Master Podcast,

100
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
I have an interview with somebody who I think you're going to be very interested to hear from.

101
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
His name is Hab Butler.

102
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
And Hab is a long time Scouter and a very active volunteer here in our council.

103
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
And over time, he has become one of the fellows who has the time and the expertise

104
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
to lend on a regional and national level,

105
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
and Hab has been involved with the New Journey to Excellence program.

106
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Saying involved, that's kind of putting it lightly.

107
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Hab chaired the committee that put together this New Journey to Excellence program.

108
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
When I saw that he was involved with that,

109
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
I saw that in Scouting Magazine.

110
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
I got in touch with him and I asked him if he would speak to me about it

111
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
because it's caused a lot of interest.

112
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:42,000
And it's a program that I'm solidly behind and I'm looking forward to applying in our troop.

113
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,000
I thought that we could all benefit from hearing Hab talk a little bit about the process

114
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
of putting it together and I was able to ask him a couple of questions about it.

115
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,000
So that interview fills the remainder of the podcast and it's a great interview.

116
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
So let's get started, shall we?

117
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
Hab Butler is joining me today on the Scoutmaster podcast.

118
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:36,000
He's presently the commissioner for the northeast region of the Boy Scouts of America

119
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
and a 2005 recipient of the Silver Buffalo Award for Distinguished Service to Youth.

120
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
If you're not familiar with the Silver Buffalo Award,

121
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,000
that is a national award that it recognizes noteworthy and extraordinary service to youth

122
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
that is national in scope.

123
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:55,000
Now I've known Hab for a number of years as probably one of the most dedicated volunteers

124
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
here in Chester County Council.

125
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
I don't think there's too many volunteer positions he hasn't held

126
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
and I'm pleased to welcome him to the Scoutmaster podcast today.

127
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,000
How are you doing, Hab?

128
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,000
I'm doing great.

129
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
I was a regional commissioner for three years.

130
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:12,000
I'm now something called the Regional Executive Vice President,

131
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:18,000
which means if I let something unusual happen,

132
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,000
it'll likely the next May I'll be elected a regional president.

133
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Oh, okay.

134
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Well, because it's three years and that's the three-year rule for you, right?

135
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Three-year rule is that for area and regional commissioners,

136
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:38,000
you're appointed for a one-year term and can be reappointed,

137
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:42,000
but not to see three years of service.

138
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:48,000
And the reason that is to allow other people to have those jobs

139
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:53,000
and bring their new energy and their new thinking

140
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and to keep the program fresh.

141
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
How did you get involved in Scouts?

142
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Well, I lived in Christiana, Pennsylvania.

143
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:09,000
My mother learned about scouting and decided it might be a pretty good thing

144
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
and she couldn't get anybody to start a club.

145
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
So she did when it came time to go to Boy Scout.

146
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,000
She couldn't find anybody to run a troupe.

147
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:29,000
And in those days, men were the only people permitted to be scoutmasters.

148
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
So she said, look, there's a scout troupe in Aglan,

149
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
and if you want to be a Boy Scout, you'll figure out how to get there.

150
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,000
So it was three or four miles away,

151
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
so I rode my bike,

152
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:46,000
or a walker, and in those days, you could enter.

153
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:51,000
So that was a very good experience in 1953.

154
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
The scoutmaster said there's something called a jamboree going to be held

155
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
in Southern California at the Irvine Range,

156
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
and the council has two scholarships.

157
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
And if anybody's interested in applying for them, there's a forum.

158
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
So I said, I'll take a forum.

159
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,000
And my friend said, you're never going to get that.

160
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Nobody knows where Akron, Pennsylvania is,

161
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
and certainly there are bigger troops and more deserving than you.

162
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
I said, well, I won't get it if I don't apply.

163
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
I went to 1953 jamboree,

164
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:32,000
and we went out two weeks by train,

165
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
one week at the jamboree, that was the beginning.

166
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
You, somewhere along the way, started volunteering as an adult?

167
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Yeah, what happened was we had two girls and two boys.

168
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
The girls were older.

169
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
So they got involved in Girl Scouts,

170
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:52,000
and then the two boys got involved in Cubs,

171
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,000
and I became the PAC committee chair.

172
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
One moved to the troop 135 in Parksburg.

173
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,000
I went there and worked with the PAC and the troop

174
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
until the other son came along,

175
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
and I was the troop committee chair there.

176
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
So they got involved in training,

177
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and they got involved in commercial work,

178
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:15,000
and they must have been absolutely desperate.

179
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
I became a chancellor commissioner and a special president.

180
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,000
I guess I'd been on the board for maybe 25 or 30 years at this point.

181
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:28,000
After that, the area director actually,

182
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,000
if I'd become the area president,

183
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
that was a terrific job.

184
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And as a result of that,

185
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:41,000
I then was later appointed to be on the Northeast Region Board.

186
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:46,000
And I've been involved in Wood Badge for quite a while,

187
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
and I've been in the course director's place,

188
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
and while I was in that role,

189
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,000
somebody in the national office decided they were going to rewrite

190
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
the Wood Badge course,

191
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:02,000
so I ended up being one of five members of the design team.

192
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:06,000
And that was for the first pilot course that was offered,

193
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and that wasn't as far as she based in 2000.

194
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,000
So later again for the final pilot course,

195
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
that was run at Rocky Mountain,

196
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and so on, later that fall,

197
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
while we got feedback,

198
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:27,000
and rewrote the program again over the next two years,

199
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and as a result of that,

200
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
I'm an exposure nationally,

201
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:35,000
and I still serve on the Finance and Investment Committee,

202
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:40,000
and serve on the National Supply Group Committee

203
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
with deals with everything you see in the boy catalog.

204
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
I'm on the Human Resources Committee.

205
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,000
I chaired the National Court of Honor,

206
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:56,000
so anybody who learns a hero award

207
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
that would come through that committee,

208
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
and anybody to use a silver beaver,

209
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:06,000
beaver, silver antelope, or silver buffalo award

210
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,000
that's managed by that committee in the Court of Honor,

211
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
so I did that for 40 years.

212
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
And most recently,

213
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,000
I'm the chairman of the Journey to Excellence

214
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
team that designed this program.

215
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Tell me what you think of the new Journey to Excellence program.

216
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
I think it started off on the right foot.

217
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
We had a team made up of professionals,

218
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,000
scouting executives, and a couple area directors,

219
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and the volunteers were been in scouting

220
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
for quite some period of time,

221
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
very knowledgeable about the program,

222
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,000
and also had knowledge of how counsel

223
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
and the district operate,

224
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,000
and we also had a couple professionals

225
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
from the National Office.

226
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:57,000
We only worked by phone and by email.

227
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
I wanted the page to be fairly quick,

228
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
and I wanted to have this,

229
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
not the expense of travel or lodging.

230
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
Well, what we did was determine

231
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
that there are five primary areas

232
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,000
that will be leading or lagging indicators of success.

233
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Those areas are finance, membership, program,

234
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
service, and governance.

235
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,000
So we had lots of good data

236
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
from the National Office database

237
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
that gave us a good sense

238
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
that we could find the things

239
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
that would differentiate,

240
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
would cancel the districts and units

241
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
from those that are struggling.

242
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:48,000
My view is that there's really a bell-shaped curve.

243
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Always have startup units

244
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
where you have brand-new leaders

245
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
that they want to excel.

246
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:55,000
They need training.

247
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
They need a lot of support early in the game.

248
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
At the other end,

249
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:04,000
those that excel and have been there for years and years,

250
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
what we ought to do is copy what they're doing

251
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
and see that other units are far aware of it.

252
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
In fact, we now have a best practices portal

253
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
on the national website.

254
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
In the middle of that bell-shaped curve

255
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
are the bulk of the units

256
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
that need help from unit commissioners.

257
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
They need help from their charter partners.

258
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:28,000
They need to have a good committee structure,

259
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
and they need that financing to make it work for them.

260
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,000
This may have been the most exciting thing I want.

261
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:37,000
Well, I'm very excited about it, Habin.

262
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:40,000
As a leader with a bit of tenure behind me,

263
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
it takes a little bit to get me excited.

264
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
What I like about it,

265
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
and I think you alluded to it earlier,

266
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:50,000
is that it's based on numbers and metrics and things like that.

267
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,000
What it does is recognize that you're doing the things

268
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
that an effective unit does.

269
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:00,000
You can't push these numbers just by pushing numbers.

270
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
You have to actually have a good program.

271
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Well, I'm glad you got into that

272
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
because that's really at the core of what Scouting's all about,

273
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,000
that this is all about the kids having a terrific experience.

274
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:17,000
It's really what it is that we're trying to do.

275
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,000
You know, sometimes folks will lose sight of that,

276
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,000
and they'll say,

277
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
we know what we were blinded last year.

278
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:27,000
We're going to do everything we possibly can to get to be silver,

279
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
and sometimes we'll get to be gold.

280
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
I hope they do have a focus on improvement.

281
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
But I hope they do.

282
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
They do not do it at the expense

283
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:40,000
of diminishing the delivery of the program to the kids.

284
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
My committee chair and I sat down and looked at the journey to excellence.

285
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
We looked at the standards and everything.

286
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
There's a spreadsheet available,

287
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,000
and you can kind of plug numbers into it and see where you're at.

288
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
And we just did it kind of off the cuff,

289
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and it all made perfect sense.

290
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Because what it actually did is I think it kind of revealed,

291
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:04,000
okay, well, we're following a little short on a couple of things.

292
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
And this makes sense.

293
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
This is not, you know,

294
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
it wasn't some kind of extraordinary thing

295
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
that was just going to push a number somewhere.

296
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
It made sense that it made our program better.

297
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:22,000
And it really did make it better for the youth members of our program.

298
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:25,000
And that is what's really got me excited about it.

299
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
Well, I was delighted to hear that.

300
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:30,000
What we'll do is periodically, not every year,

301
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:35,000
but periodically we'll look at what the leading and lagging indicators are

302
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
to say are we measuring the right stuff.

303
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:43,000
And part of the way we're going to do that is to see what the scores are,

304
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
how they vary from year to year,

305
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:50,000
but tomorrow I will be on a phone call

306
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
for probably about two hours

307
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:57,000
where we will be discussing something that will be added

308
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
to the journey to excellence program

309
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:02,000
around the beginning of the year.

310
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
It's called The Voice of the Customer.

311
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
What we need to do is,

312
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
we're from parents that will get surveyed

313
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:14,000
to provide commentary on, you know,

314
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
what do we need to know if we're measuring the wrong things

315
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:21,000
we need to be aware of what our customer base is

316
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:25,000
and thinking of us and figure out how we move from there.

317
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,000
There's a really deep overall commitment

318
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
from all of our national leadership

319
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
to this journey to excellence concept.

320
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
And I think that's reflected in the most recent issue

321
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:38,000
of Scouting Magazine, where they talk about it.

322
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:43,000
This is going to be a real driver for the entire BSA

323
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
in the foreseeable future.

324
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:49,000
Well, I'll tell you, I had in the back of my mind

325
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:53,000
that at some time we would find that this model

326
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
could be used in other parts of Scouting.

327
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:00,000
I would say in January of this year,

328
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
one of the top scatters who was on the National OA

329
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
Executive Committee called and said,

330
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
I need to talk with you about this journey to excellence.

331
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:12,000
And I have a funny feeling that we could use

332
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
the journey to excellence model and principles

333
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:19,000
for a journey of excellence recognition for...

334
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
What do you think about that?

335
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
Because of the amount of respect I have for a person

336
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
called, I'm not sure if it thinks it's okay.

337
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:30,000
If Clark Green thinks it's okay, we must.

338
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:35,000
Well, I've heard lots of positive feedback about it.

339
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:42,000
I wanted to look at some of the 13 different criteria in here.

340
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,000
One that immediately drew my attention was leadership training.

341
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
And I don't mean this in a negative way,

342
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
but that's a pretty stiff requirement.

343
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:56,000
When you think about it from the standpoint of the kids,

344
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
we're talking about doing our best.

345
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
We're talking about doing it in a safe environment.

346
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:04,000
We have lots of people who say we're the premier youth

347
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
leadership organization in the world.

348
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
Well, in order to do that,

349
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:12,000
leaders don't become leaders by having low standards.

350
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:15,000
And it's easier now than it has been any time

351
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
since I became involved with Scouting

352
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:20,000
to become a trained leader because of all the online resources

353
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
and everything.

354
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,000
Now, I think that should be a pretty tough standard

355
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
because it drives so much of the other things

356
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:32,000
that indicate a quality experience for Scouts.

357
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:36,000
The other thing that drew my attention was the patrol method.

358
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
Boy, I can't tell you how pleased I was to see that

359
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:43,000
because I think it's something that doesn't get enough emphasis

360
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,000
in other places in our program.

361
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:51,000
But the way that you've built the implementation

362
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,000
of the patrol method in here and the senior patrol leader,

363
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:58,000
I'm really appreciative of that.

364
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
Well, thank you.

365
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:04,000
One of the ways you get paid in Scouting

366
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
when you spend an awful lot of time

367
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:10,000
is you get to meet a lot of people all across the country.

368
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:13,000
Lots of them are programmed rather than administrative types.

369
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:18,000
You listen carefully as to what the key element of success

370
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:21,000
in a Scouting unit is.

371
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
It is absolutely the patrol method.

372
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:28,000
Nothing is as important as the patrol method

373
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:36,000
and maintaining the integrity of the position of SPL, especially.

374
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:41,000
When you see a boy named to be SPL

375
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:44,000
and you just talk with him for a few minutes,

376
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:45,000
you say to yourself,

377
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:48,000
wow, what a good decision this was.

378
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:52,000
The tiered ratings, the gold, silver and bronze ratings,

379
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
is that going to cause a whole lot of friction

380
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
or competition amongst units, do you think?

381
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:01,000
Especially when it comes to Webullos, parents looking at units,

382
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:04,000
are they only going to go shopping now for the gold units?

383
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:07,000
Is this fair to a smaller troop

384
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:10,000
that might not be able to hit that?

385
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:14,000
That's certainly a concern because we want everybody to win.

386
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:17,000
But winning comes in different forms.

387
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:26,000
While parents shop more in areas where we can shop

388
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:30,000
for the units for their boy to go to,

389
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:36,000
that's not changed as a result of the journey that's been around

390
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:40,000
where people will choose a troop for their son

391
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,000
or a son will choose for all kinds of reasons.

392
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:46,000
You have a great troop and you have one that's not so great.

393
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:50,000
The best troop is always looking for ways to improve.

394
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:54,000
And a startup unit or the one that may have floundered

395
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
had to change the leadership

396
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:59,000
and seeing that your scores aren't as good

397
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
as some of the other units,

398
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
I think that would drive people to say,

399
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:04,000
that's what you said.

400
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:07,000
We have a spot or two here where we can improve.

401
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:11,000
Not everybody is going to be at the top of the pack every year.

402
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:14,000
We'll all go through a picture and balance.

403
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
You mentioned this a little bit,

404
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:20,000
but a smaller troop of 20 or less scouts

405
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and a big troop of 50 or more,

406
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:26,000
are they going to get scored basically the same way with us

407
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:30,000
or is it slanted so that it's easier for a large troop?

408
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:34,000
They are going to be scored on the same criteria.

409
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:37,000
What we will do is take a look at this

410
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:40,000
and see if there are differences.

411
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:41,000
We don't anticipate.

412
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
We're going to see a significant difference.

413
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
There's one thing I didn't say

414
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
in the early part of this conversation.

415
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
This troop I was in in Akland, Pennsylvania,

416
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:52,000
which was a new troop 20.

417
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
When I was there,

418
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
we never had more than 12 kids.

419
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
When I tell people,

420
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,000
I only claim the thing was I was a senior patrol leader

421
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
in Akland, Pennsylvania.

422
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
I had the best scouting experience

423
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
that anybody could deliver.

424
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
I didn't feel that I missed much.

425
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
The program, the Journey to Excellence,

426
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:15,000
doesn't give any advantage to a smaller troop or a bigger troop.

427
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:18,000
Basically, you're doing percentages

428
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
and incremental improvements

429
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:23,000
to up your score as you go on.

430
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,000
As a boy goes through the ranks

431
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
and learns more and does more

432
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:31,000
and gets recognized and grows,

433
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:35,000
that's what we hope will happen with yours.

434
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:40,000
I certainly appreciate all the work that went into it

435
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
and all the thinking that went into it.

436
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:45,000
I think it's being overwhelmingly well-received.

437
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:48,000
You guys are going to keep working on this.

438
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
We are.

439
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
I talked with you about term limits

440
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:55,000
on the area and the regional commissioners.

441
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
At some point in time,

442
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
I should be replaced.

443
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:02,000
Somebody with French thinking should come in

444
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
and share this.

445
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:07,000
The members of the task force,

446
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:11,000
we should all be giving a thank you for your service letter

447
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:15,000
and let it go for a year or two

448
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,000
and start a new group

449
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:21,000
and go in and look at all the data

450
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,000
and figure out where it's working

451
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
and where it needs to be improved.

452
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:28,000
This program needs to be subjected

453
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
to continuous improvement.

454
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
I wanted to ask you a couple other questions

455
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
since you're involved at a regional and national level

456
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
on a lot of things.

457
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
I don't know if you ever noticed this

458
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
and I say this kind of tongue in cheek,

459
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
but every once in a while the rank and file,

460
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:45,000
the scout masters and assistant scout masters

461
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
and cub masters and things like that

462
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:49,000
seem to get a little frustrated

463
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:53,000
with what goes on up at these national levels and things.

464
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:57,000
They feel sometimes they're just a little disconnected from them.

465
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:00,000
I don't know if you ever encountered that or not.

466
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:03,000
I sure have. I was one of those people.

467
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
I am one.

468
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
I plan to train.

469
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
They say what needs to be said

470
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:13,000
and frankly that's a great segue

471
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:16,000
again to this voice of the customer

472
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
because voices are scattered rather

473
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:23,000
because there'll be opportunities for people to say,

474
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:26,000
you know what, I think you're absolutely nuts.

475
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:30,000
Why would anybody do this or why are you not doing this?

476
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:35,000
So I'll at the risk of becoming even busier.

477
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:38,000
Anytime anybody has something on their mind

478
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
they can't get anybody to listen.

479
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:43,000
Give them my phone number or my e-mail address.

480
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
If I'm not the person,

481
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:48,000
I'll find somebody to put them in touch with.

482
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:51,000
People at national and regional levels of scouting,

483
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:52,000
the volunteers there,

484
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:55,000
they have any interest at all in what a little guy has to say,

485
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:58,000
a unit leader or committee person has to say?

486
00:26:58,000 --> 00:26:59,000
Absolutely.

487
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
The only reason you have areas

488
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:04,000
and the region involved

489
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:07,000
is to help councils do a better job

490
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:10,000
of supporting units.

491
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:16,000
We're not left with an overabundance of volunteers

492
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
anywhere in the organization

493
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:21,000
but I've never been part of a volunteer organization

494
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:23,000
where there have been too many good people.

495
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:26,000
It doesn't work that way, unfortunately.

496
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
If you think about what Baden Powell

497
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:31,000
thought scouting could do for the world

498
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:33,000
and I think he's right

499
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:36,000
that if we play this game right

500
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
we can get to world peace

501
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:42,000
and then we will have plenty of people to help us.

502
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:45,000
We need to do the best we can

503
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:47,000
with our scouting jobs

504
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:50,000
but not at the expense of our families

505
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:53,000
and our jobs and what else we do in the community.

506
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:56,000
We need to let other people in the community

507
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:59,000
know how important this program is

508
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
for the benefit of the kids

509
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:03,000
and their communities

510
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
and our country and frankly the world

511
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:10,000
in total and invite them to become part of it.

512
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
A few of us, like I said,

513
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:15,000
the frontline volunteers

514
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:17,000
actually get to meet anybody

515
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:20,000
in a regional or national position.

516
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
I think a lot of times people don't think

517
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:25,000
that they're heard or listened to

518
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:27,000
or thought of very much.

519
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:30,000
You've gone from being that cubmaster

520
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
and doing all that

521
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:35,000
to being involved in these regional

522
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:37,000
and national decisions now

523
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:40,000
so you're telling us that people do actually hear

524
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:43,000
and they do and it is important to them.

525
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
I just looked at a video tape

526
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:48,000
done by a guy named Rex Tillerson

527
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
who's the national president.

528
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
Tillerson is also the CEO

529
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
of the world's largest company

530
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
called the Exxon Meble

531
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:59,000
and Rex Tillerson's father

532
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:04,000
was a scout camp ranger.

533
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:08,000
Tillerson's son is a district executive

534
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:12,000
and when he talks about scouting

535
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:15,000
he talks about the main thing with the passion

536
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:18,000
and the main thing is to deliver this program

537
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
to more kids and system.

538
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
He goes all over the country

539
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
while he's still working for Exxon Meble

540
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:28,000
and I've had a couple conversations with him

541
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
I think to myself

542
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
I can't believe I'm talking with this guy

543
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
who runs a world's largest company

544
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
and he's listening to me.

545
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
He said,

546
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:41,000
what do I need to know that nobody's telling me?

547
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:45,000
Well, there's lots of Rex Tillerson

548
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:48,000
who wants to know

549
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:50,000
and all they need to recognize

550
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:53,000
Rome wasn't built in the day

551
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:56,000
and that we can't change everything

552
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:59,000
we want to change as quickly as we like

553
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:02,000
because of resource limitation

554
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:05,000
whether it be money or time or talent

555
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:07,000
or facilities or what have you

556
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:09,000
everybody should feel

557
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
and should know

558
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,000
that there's lots of people who want to hear

559
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:15,000
what needs to be said.

560
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,000
What do we need to do

561
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,000
so we get more kids in the program?

562
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,000
You or anybody else who ever has

563
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:25,000
something that needs to be said

564
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
and there's no one to listen

565
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:29,000
for him to say

566
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:31,000
something to work, send an email.

567
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:33,000
So is that permission

568
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:35,000
to put your email address

569
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,000
a link to your email address

570
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,000
on my website

571
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,000
where this podcast will be then?

572
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:44,000
You can put my cell phone number in there too.

573
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
I don't think I'll put your cell phone number there.

574
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
I don't know if that's a great idea

575
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:50,000
but I will put your email address.

576
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:52,000
You're certainly welcome to.

577
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,000
I'll give you some more good news

578
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:56,000
like

579
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,000
most organizations that I've worked in

580
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
their information

581
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:02,000
technology and delivery

582
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:04,000
departments

583
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:06,000
are always

584
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:08,000
we've got more requests

585
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:10,000
for stuff to be done

586
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:12,000
than the capabilities of doing.

587
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:14,000
So in the next two years

588
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:16,000
you're going to see the BSA

589
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:18,000
develop

590
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,000
an entirely new platform

591
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:22,000
which should

592
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:24,000
significantly improve

593
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,000
the way

594
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,000
information is shared

595
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,000
but we're getting there

596
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,000
but we have a long, long way to go

597
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
and that's very good news

598
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
to know that that's happening

599
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:38,000
and it's being looked at

600
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:40,000
and it will take a lot of time

601
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,000
but I'm willing to wait a couple years

602
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:44,000
but no more than a couple years

603
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:46,000
so you need to get on that.

604
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:48,000
Thank you.

605
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:50,000
I'll be in Dallas

606
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:52,000
October

607
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:54,000
10th through the 12th

608
00:31:54,000 --> 00:31:56,000
I think it is

609
00:31:56,000 --> 00:31:58,000
and I'll be talking with the person

610
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:00,000
who runs the IT department

611
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:02,000
and I will absolutely

612
00:32:02,000 --> 00:32:04,000
pass on

613
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,000
not only my thoughts

614
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,000
but the guy I've known

615
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:10,000
in the distance

616
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:12,000
and I opened up the opportunity

617
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:14,000
to say tell me what we need to hear

618
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:16,000
I need to and one of the things

619
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:18,000
you know get moving here

620
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:20,000
so great.

621
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:22,000
Well we're always going to be a little bit impatient

622
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:24,000
but

623
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:26,000
I think any of us

624
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:28,000
who have tried to run a camperee

625
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:30,000
or any kind of a district event

626
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:32,000
we understand

627
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:34,000
at least in some respects

628
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:36,000
as soon as you start adding numbers to it

629
00:32:36,000 --> 00:32:38,000
it slows down a little bit

630
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:40,000
and it gets just a tiny bit more difficult to manage

631
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:42,000
so I can only imagine

632
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:44,000
what's happening to our poor IT guy

633
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:46,000
down in Dallas there

634
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:48,000
we've got a new leader and there's nothing like a new leader

635
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:50,000
Well I really appreciate you spending time

636
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:52,000
with me today

637
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:54,000
maybe we can do it again sometime

638
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:56,000
Anytime you'd like

639
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:58,000
I'd be glad to

640
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:00,000
Your email is going to go on the blog

641
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:02,000
so watch your inbox there

642
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:04,000
Good deal Clark

643
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:06,000
Thanks very much

644
